-Given a differentiably parametrized family of policies $\{ \pi_\theta\}_\theta$, for a MDP, we can optimize the parameters to maximize the expected value under that policy. One such class of methods are called policy gradient methods; they're based on stochastic gradient optimization of the hard-to-compute return function. The most basic such algorithm is called REINFORCE. Given a $\theta$ we want to nudge it to improve expected value under the parametrized policy. We will find an easy to compute unbiased estimator of the return under $\pi_\theta$ given actions and states and do stochastic gradient optimization using that. We sample a sequence of states and actions $S_t$ and $A_t$ (an episode), following the policy $\pi_\theta$, we call the future value from timestep $t$ of such a sequence $G_t$. Thus $G_t$ is an unbiased estimator of $Q_{\pi_\theta}(S_t, A_t)$. Then we use $G_t \nabla_\theta \log(\pi_\theta(S_t, A_t)$ as our gradient, where intuitively log corrects for the fact that we want to nudge rarely seen $(S_t,A_t)$ more since we'll be nudging them less often (there is a mathematical derivation). We could use standard stochastic gradient ascent, but the assignment specifies the Adam optimizer. Hence given a stepsize $\alpha > 0$ use the gradient as input into the Adam optimizer, for each $(S_t,A_t)$ in the sequence used to compute $G_t$.
+\textbf{REINFORCE}
+Given a differentiably parametrized family of policies $\{ \pi_\theta\}_\theta$, for a MDP, we can optimize the parameters to maximize the expected value under that policy. One such class of methods are called policy gradient methods; they're based on stochastic gradient optimization of the hard-to-compute return function. The most basic such algorithm is called REINFORCE. Given a $\theta$ we want to nudge it to improve expected value under the parametrized policy. We will find an easy to compute unbiased estimator of the gradient of expected discounted return under $\pi_\theta$ given actions and states and do stochastic gradient optimization using that. We sample a sequence of states and actions $S_t$ and $A_t$ (an episode), following the policy $\pi_\theta$, we call the future value from timestep $t$ of such a sequence $G_t$. Thus $G_t$ is an unbiased estimator of $Q_{\pi_\theta}(S_t, A_t)$. Then we use terms $\gamma^tG_t\nabla_\theta\log\pi_\theta(A_t\mid S_t)$ in our gradient. The log comes from the identity $\nabla_\theta\pi_\theta=\pi_\theta\nabla_\theta\log\pi_\theta$. We could use standard stochastic gradient ascent, but the assignment specifies the Adam optimizer. Hence given a stepsize $\alpha>0$, we sum the discounted gradient terms within each episode, average over the batch of episodes, and use the result for one Adam update. The objective is $\mathbb E[\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\gamma^t r_t]$, where $T$ is the episode length and $r_t$ its reward after decision $t$. Our code uses a fixed gradient scale of 0.05 for CartPole and 1 for Pong; it does not standardize returns. Gradient-norm caps are 10 and 5 respectively, so the clipped estimator need not remain unbiased.
