+Here we use a scalar exponential moving average. Batch $k$ uses the existing $b_k$, starting at $b_0=0$. After the actor update, we use this batch's returns to compute
+\[\bar G_k=\frac{\sum_{i,t}\gamma^tG_t^{(i)}}{\sum_{i,t}\gamma^t},\qquad b_{k+1}=0.95b_k+0.05\bar G_k.\]
+The index $i$ runs over all eight games, and $t$ is the decision within each game. Thus it is a discount-weighted average of reward-to-go over the batch, not a second return calculation or a value fitted separately for each state. 
+
+
+The plot is fairly similar to the one without a baseline. The final result was $-18.446\pm1.851$ over 500 games, only 0.164 above the basic method; all were losses. This does not establish a benefit across training seeds or lower gradient variance.
