-If the rewards for all actions are 1 and the games terminate, then the baselines should converge to 1, and our gradients should be 0. This is unlike the case without a baseline.
+If the rewards are all 0, then the target returns are 0 and a fitted baseline should approach 0. We tested this in both cases. Fitting the CartPole baseline for 400 Adam steps on 64 modified episodes gave held-out MSE $\ZeroFitMSE$ on 64 separate episodes. Pong's EMA stayed exactly 0 when initialized at 0; from 2 it decayed to \ZeroEMA{} after 160 zero-return updates, matching $2(0.95)^{160}$. The latter is a fixed-data baseline-mechanics experiment, not a new on-policy performance run.
+\par\end{samepage}
+
+Setting all the baselines to 0 recovers REINFORCE. On identical data, the maximum gradient difference was exactly 0 in both implementations. Zero returns with zero baseline also gave gradient norm 0.
+
+We made sure to fit our baseline after updating the policy, so its predictions for the current actor update use weights from earlier data. Fitting on the current outcomes first can introduce dependence on the current actions, even if actions are not explicit network inputs.
+
+If an episode lasts only one decision and every action receives reward 1, then all returns are 1. With an exact baseline of 1, both actor implementations gave gradient norm 0. Fitting the CartPole predictor on one-step episodes gave held-out mean \OneFitMean{} and MSE $\OneFitMSE$; Pong's EMA approached \OneEMA{} after 160 updates from 0. Finite fitting is approximate, so exact cancellation is checked separately with the baseline fixed to 1. Without a baseline, individual sampled gradients can be nonzero, although their expectation is still zero because every action has the same payoff. For longer episodes with reward 1 at every decision, returns exceed 1, so the target baseline need not be 1.
+
+Finally, we checked whether baselines actually reduced gradient variance. At a frozen initial CartPole actor, fitting a separate baseline on 512 episodes and testing on 512 different paired episodes reduced summed gradient-component variance from 5.338262 to 0.842421 (84.22\%). This used 150 fitting steps, rather than the five per training batch. Pong's independently fitted initial constant reduced variance from 369.320 to 20.763 on 16 held-out games, but the actual EMA's later variance ratios were 0.873, 1.017, 0.924 and 1.011. Thus variance reduction did not persist at every checkpoint; these probes measure unclipped gradients, and 16 games gives noisy estimates.
 
 \subsection{3 More Months}
+We would compare constant, state-dependent and time-dependent baselines on identical frozen policies, using separate fitting data and more held-out episodes. We would track baseline error and gradient variance throughout training, with uncertainty intervals, and separate the effect of clipping from baseline subtraction. This would test when lower gradient variance actually produces better control.
